
Tests are grouped under `FS1` through `FS6` to match the requirements. PlatformIO
test-suite directories inside each group start with `test_`, for example:

```text
test/FS3/test_leds/test_main.cpp
```

List available suites with `pio test -e nanoatmega328new --list-tests`.
Hardware suites are real diagnostic firmware: run only when ready to flash the
Nano, connect the required components, and observe the outputs. For example,
`pio test -e nanoatmega328new -f FS3/test_leds` runs the LED color walk.

| Group | Test suites | Main coverage |
|---|---|---|
| FS1 | `test_pulse_detector`, `test_sensor` | PPG peak detection, noisy/slow/fast signals, analog WPSE340 signal |
| FS2 | `test_rtc` | RTC validity, calendar fields and advancing seconds |
| FS3 | `test_heart_rate_indicators`, `test_leds` | BPM classification and one-at-a-time physical LED color check |
| FS4 | `test_heartbeat_audio`, `test_buzzer` | Tone selection, simulated beat cadence and physical D2 mute/re-enable |
| FS5 | `test_pulse_graph`, `test_pulse_graph_zoom`, `test_displays` | Graph ring buffer/scaling, seconds/zoom calculations, both OLEDs and encoder |
| FS6 | `test_measurement_store`, `test_eeprom_hardware` | EEPROM persistence logic, averaging, recovery and device capacity |

See [FS_TEST_PLAN.md](FS_TEST_PLAN.md) for requirement-by-requirement test
coverage and the physical procedures needed for requirements that cannot be
proven by unit tests alone.

Host-only suites use Unity and can be compiled with a host C++11 compiler. They
must be run separately from the hardware suites; see the test plan for the
current host command.
